From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tiberiu Chibici <chibicitiberiu@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 13:06:30 +0300
Subject: nv30: less work per draw (texture and vertex caches, vertex formats,
 constants, texture units)

Six changes to what nv30 sends and does per draw, measured together in Mesa ps3.262 (every
suite game 11-24% faster except Armagetron, whose loss came from 0019). Each one leaves out
writes that change nothing on the GPU, or work on the PPE whose result is the same.

Texture cache: nv30_state_validate ended every draw with the texture cache invalidate the
NVIDIA blob was traced doing (TEX_CACHE_CTL 2, then 1: the vertex and the fragment texture
caches, then three 0x1718 PIPE_NOPs), ten words in the push buffer, ten more for the kernel to
copy and check, and a flush of the GPU's texture caches before each draw. libgcm leaves the
invalidate to the application, after it changed texels (docs/RSX-PROGRAMS.md); binding other
textures needs none. The screen now counts the events after which texture memory may hold
something the cache doesn't (tex_gen, bumped atomically by any context): a copy into or out of
a texture (nv30_transfer_rect: uploads, readbacks, copies, the 3D blit), a new framebuffer
(what was rendered may be sampled from now on; also after a context switch, which revalidates
it), and a flush (another context of the process, on the same channel, may sample what this
one rendered). A context emits the same sequence as before, at the same place, before its
first draw after the count moved. ps3rsx already invalidates both caches whenever another
channel ran (shadow.c), so other processes' rendering and uploads are covered. nv30 doesn't
expose texture barriers, so sampling a bound render target isn't defined anyway.
NV30_TEX_INVALIDATE_ALWAYS=1 restores the invalidate before every draw, to tell a stale
texture from another bug on the PS3: watch for a texture showing what it held before
(just-uploaded glyphs, minimap or atlas updates, render-to-texture effects).

Vertex cache: nv30_state_validate invalidates the vertex cache (0x1710) in every draw, after
the last write any vertex or index buffer of the draw could have had (CPU writes go before the
draw call, coherent mappings included). nv30_draw_vbo then emitted another one, right before
the primitive, whenever a buffer had been written since the last draw (vbo_dirty) or a bound
buffer was mapped coherently, and for the latter walked the vertex buffers every draw to find
out. Nothing writes vertex memory between the two, so the second one invalidated an empty
cache: it is dropped, with the walk. Two words saved in every draw that follows a vertex or
index buffer write, which with u_vbuf uploading user arrays and Mesa's immediate-mode buffers
is most draws of GL 1.x games.

Vertex formats: nv30_vbo_validate runs whenever the vertex buffers or elements change, and
wrote the whole VTXFMT burst each time: one word per attribute plus the header. The formats
depend only on the vertex elements (their formats and strides) and on whether vertices are
pushed inline, not on the buffers, and Mesa's state tracker rebinds vertex buffers far more
often than it changes the element layout (u_vbuf binds a new upload buffer for user arrays in
every draw). The context now keeps the VTXFMT words the GPU holds (state.vtxfmt, carried over a
context switch with the rest of nv30->state; the swtnl path, the only other writer, records
its own) and leaves the burst out when it would write the same words. ps3rsx shadows VTXFMT
and puts it back after another channel ran, and the offsets (VTXBUF) are written as before.

Constants: the state tracker hands nv30 its shader constants as user memory, again whenever
they change, in fixed-function GL about every draw. nv30_set_constant_buffer wrapped each such
pointer in a new nv04_resource and freed the previous wrapper, an allocation and a free on the
PPE per update. Only the wrapper's data pointer and size are ever read (vertex and fragment
program validation, the swtnl path), and nothing but the context's constbuf slot references a
wrapper. While the slot still holds the wrapper made by the last call and nothing else
references it, it is now pointed at the new memory instead; a real constant buffer, or a
wrapper someone else holds on to, takes the old path. Constants are uploaded exactly as before.

Texture units: the state tracker passes nv30 all its sampler views (and binds all its
samplers) when any of them changes. nv30 marked every unit dirty and dropped every unit's
buffer reference, so nv30_fragtex_validate rewrote each one: 13 words and two buffer
references per unit, for units that kept what they had. A unit is now marked only when its
view or its sampler is a different object. The context holds a reference to each bound view,
so an equal pointer is the same view of the same texture; sampler CSOs aren't deleted while
bound. The 3D blit marks the unit it uses, as before. A context switch (two contexts of a
process on the same channel) marked everything dirty but validated only the units changed
since the context last drew, so a context could sample the other one's textures; it now marks
all the context's units.

Vertex program constants: nv30_vertprog_validate sent every changed constant on its own
(VP_UPLOAD_CONST_ID, the slot, its four words: six words per constant), and a matrix is four
consecutive slots. The GPU moves to the next slot after every four words of a burst (libgcm
uploads up to eight constants per burst; ps3rsx's shadow follows the same rule), so a run of
changed constants in consecutive slots now goes up as one burst of at most four, the
VP_UPLOAD_CONST window the method table documents. The same constant writes, six words less
per matrix.

(0020 to 0025 until they were folded, 2026-10-31; their switches other than
NV30_TEX_INVALIDATE_ALWAYS, which brought back each old path, went with the fold.)

Forwarded: no

